📄 1.1 — Executive Summary
Labels: overview business-case
Parent: Overview & Business Case

One Line: We are replacing EC2-backed ECS compute for DBT with AWS Fargate — eliminating infrastructure management, improving tenant isolation, and aligning cost to actual usage.

Why This Change
The current DBT platform runs ECS tasks on EC2 instances managed within an Auto Scaling Group. This works, but it forces the platform team to operate a layer of infrastructure — EC2 instances, AMIs, Auto Scaling Groups — that provides no value for a short-lived batch workload like DBT. Every DBT run is a container that starts, executes, and stops. The EC2 layer underneath it just needs to disappear.
What We’re Doing
We are migrating DBT execution to AWS Fargate. Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers. We define CPU, memory, networking, and IAM requirements — AWS manages everything underneath. No instances. No patching. No capacity planning.
Each tenant gets a completely isolated execution environment: their own IAM roles, their own Step Functions state machine, their own Service Catalog product, and their own Fargate task at runtime. Nothing is shared.
PoC Results
The PoC was completed successfully. All validation tests passed. DBT ran correctly on Fargate, logs streamed to CloudWatch, outputs landed in S3, and IAM boundaries held across tenants.
What We’re Asking For
	1.	Approval to begin Phase 1 (Foundation work)
	2.	Approval for parallel running with two pilot tenants
	3.	Approval to decommission the EC2 cluster post-validation
	4.	Engineering capacity allocation for a 6-week rollout

📄 1.2 — Problem Statement — Why We’re Moving Away from EC2
Labels: problem-statement ec2 context
Parent: Overview & Business Case

Background
DBT runs are short-lived batch jobs. A tenant triggers a run, a container spins up, models are executed against the data warehouse, and the container exits. The entire lifecycle is typically minutes long.
Despite this simplicity, the current platform requires the platform team to maintain a full EC2-based ECS cluster underneath it. That cluster has operational demands entirely disproportionate to the workload it’s serving.

Problems with the Current EC2 Setup
AMI Lifecycle
Every EC2 instance in the cluster runs an AMI that must be regularly refreshed — OS patches, security updates, dependency changes. Each refresh requires building a new AMI, testing it, rolling it out through the ASG, draining old instances, and verifying the cluster recovers cleanly. This is engineering time spent on infrastructure, not product.
Auto Scaling Group Complexity
The ASG requires ongoing configuration: scale-in/scale-out policies, warm pools, launch templates, instance refresh strategies, and cooldown tuning. Any misconfiguration can leave the cluster under-provisioned during peak runs or over-provisioned (and overspending) during quiet periods.
Capacity Planning
The cluster must be sized for worst-case concurrent load across all tenants. In practice, this means the cluster is over-provisioned most of the time. Idle EC2 instances continue billing whether or not any DBT tasks are running.
Multi-Tenant Contention
All tenants share the same EC2 instance pool. A large DBT run from one tenant competes for resources with every other tenant scheduled at the same time. There is no hard isolation boundary — only ECS scheduling.
Instance Failure Risk
EC2 instances fail. When they do, the cluster must drain the instance, replace it, re-register it, and restore capacity. This process is operational overhead that has nothing to do with the DBT product.
Security Surface
Shared EC2 instances mean a shared host. If a container were to escape its sandbox, the shared host is the next layer of exposure — and all other tenants’ workloads run on the same host.

The Core Conclusion
The EC2 layer is infrastructure overhead with no unique architectural value for this workload. DBT runs are stateless, short-lived, and containerised. The compute substrate can and should be invisible — which is exactly what Fargate provides.

📄 1.3 — What Is AWS Fargate?
Labels: fargate explainer serverless
Parent: Overview & Business Case

Summary
AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers. You define what your container needs — CPU, memory, networking, IAM role. AWS provisions the compute, runs the container, and cleans up when the task completes. You never see an EC2 instance.

How It Works
When a Fargate task is launched:
	1.	AWS provisions isolated compute infrastructure for that task
	2.	The container image is pulled from ECR
	3.	The task runs with a dedicated ENI (its own network interface), its own CPU and memory allocation, and an AWS-managed runtime environment
	4.	When the task exits, the compute is immediately released
	5.	Billing stops at that moment
There is no host to SSH into, no AMI to maintain, no instance to drain. The compute exists only for the duration of the task.

Key Fargate Properties



|Property     |Detail                                                    |
|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
|Compute model|Serverless — AWS manages all underlying infrastructure    |
|Network mode |awsvpc mandatory — dedicated ENI per task                 |
|Billing      |Per-second vCPU and memory, from task start to task stop  |
|Isolation    |Full task-level isolation — no shared host                |
|Scaling      |Per-task — no cluster scaling required                    |
|Max task size|16 vCPU, 120GB memory                                     |
|Integration  |Native with ECS, Step Functions, IAM, CloudWatch, ECR, VPC|

Why It’s the Right Fit for DBT
DBT runs are batch jobs — they start, execute SQL transformations, and stop. They are not latency-sensitive. They don’t need persistent state on the compute layer. They don’t require host-level capabilities. They run in containers.
Fargate was designed for exactly this pattern: short-lived, containerised, on-demand workloads where infrastructure management would be pure overhead.

