📄 2.2 — Resources Created (Per Tenant)
Labels: resources iam ecs per-tenant
Parent: Architecture

Overview
Every tenant onboarded to the platform receives its own independent set of AWS resources. This table describes each resource, its type, and its purpose.



|Resource                        |AWS Service        |Purpose                                                                                      |
|--------------------------------|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|SM IAM Role                     |IAM                |Step Functions execution role — grants `ecs:RunTask` and `iam:PassRole` scoped to this tenant|
|ECS Task IAM Role               |IAM                |Runtime role assumed by the Fargate container — access to tenant S3 buckets, CloudWatch, SSM |
|ECS Execution Role              |IAM                |Allows Fargate to pull the container image from ECR and stream logs to CloudWatch            |
|ECS Log Group                   |CloudWatch         |Captures all DBT execution output for this tenant — isolated from other tenants              |
|ECS Cluster                     |ECS                |Logical cluster used for task scheduling — no EC2 instances attached                         |
|Task Definition                 |ECS                |Specifies container image, CPU, memory, network mode, IAM roles, and environment variables   |
|Task Definition SSM Parameter   |SSM Parameter Store|Stores the task definition ARN — consumed by the Service Catalog product at launch time      |
|ECS-TASK Service Catalog Product|Service Catalog    |Tenant-facing entry point — accepts input parameters and triggers the DBT execution flow     |
|Step Functions State Machine    |Step Functions     |Orchestrates the DBT execution — calls ECS RunTask and manages state transitions             |
|ECR Repository                  |ECR                |Tenant-specific container registry — stores the synced DBT image for this tenant             |

Why Per-Tenant Resources Matter
Sharing any of these resources across tenants would introduce coupling — a change in one tenant’s configuration could affect another’s execution. Dedicated resources per tenant ensure:
	∙	Configuration changes are tenant-scoped and safe to make independently
	∙	IAM permissions are bounded to the tenant’s own resources
	∙	A failure in one tenant’s state machine or task definition has no effect on others
	∙	Audit logs (CloudWatch, CloudTrail) are naturally separated per tenant

📄 2.3 — Runtime Flow — Step by Step
Labels: runtime step-functions fargate flow
Parent: Architecture

Trigger
A tenant (or an automated scheduler) invokes the ECS-TASK Service Catalog product with two parameters:
	∙	TaskDefinitionSSM — the SSM parameter path pointing to this tenant’s task definition ARN
	∙	SynchNexusImage — flag indicating whether to sync the Nexus image to ECR before execution

Execution Steps



|Step|Actor               |Action                                                                                                          |
|----|--------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|1   |Service Catalog     |Product is launched with tenant parameters                                                                      |
|2   |Step Functions      |State Machine is triggered using the SM IAM Role                                                                |
|3   |Step Functions → ECS|State Machine calls `ecs:RunTask`, passing the task definition ARN and network config (subnets, security groups)|
|4   |ECS Control Plane   |Validates the Task Definition; selects Fargate as the launch type                                               |
|5   |Fargate             |AWS provisions an isolated task: dedicated ENI, isolated CPU/memory, AWS-managed runtime                        |
|6   |Execution Role      |Fargate assumes the Execution Role and pulls the DBT image from the tenant ECR repository                       |
|7   |DBT Container       |Container starts; DBT models execute against the target data warehouse                                          |
|8   |CloudWatch          |Fargate streams container stdout/stderr to the tenant CloudWatch Log Group in real time                         |
|9   |S3                  |DBT outputs (run results, artifacts) are written to tenant S3 as configured                                     |
|10  |Task Completion     |Task transitions to STOPPED; compute is released; billing ceases                                                |

State Machine Flow (Simplified)

[START]
   ↓
[Resolve Task Definition from SSM]
   ↓
[ecs:RunTask → Fargate]
   ↓
[Wait for Task Completion]
   ↓
[Check Exit Code]
   ↓                    ↓
[SUCCESS → END]    [FAILURE → Retry / Alert]


📄 3.1 — ECS on EC2 vs ECS on Fargate
