|Dimension                |ECS on EC2                                                 |ECS on Fargate                                       |
|-------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|
|Infrastructure Management|EC2 instances, AMIs, ASGs, and instance lifecycle to manage|Fully managed by AWS — zero EC2 infrastructure       |
|Capacity Planning        |Must pre-provision cluster capacity for peak load          |No capacity planning — provisioned on demand per task|
|Compute Isolation        |Containers share underlying EC2 instances                  |Each task: dedicated ENI, CPU, memory, runtime       |
|Scaling                  |EC2 scale-out/scale-in required                            |Scales per task — no cluster changes                 |
|Billing                  |Per-hour EC2 regardless of task activity                   |Per-second vCPU + memory per task                    |
|Security                 |Shared host between tenant workloads                       |No shared host — full task-level isolation           |
|Operational Overhead     |High — patching, AMI builds, ASG tuning                    |Low — container and task definition only             |
|Multi-Tenancy            |Noisy neighbour risk; resource contention                  |Full per-task isolation by design                    |
|Cold Start               |Fast (warm EC2 already in cluster)                         |~10–30s (acceptable for batch workloads)             |
|DBT Fit                  |Workable but operationally heavy                           |Ideal — serverless, isolated, right-sized            |

Verdict
For a short-lived batch workload like DBT, the EC2 layer adds operational cost with no architectural benefit. Fargate eliminates that entire layer and replaces it with compute that is invisible, isolated, and billed to the second.

