📄 3.3 — IAM Role Structure
Labels: iam security roles least-privilege
Parent: Technical Deep Dive

Three Roles Per Tenant
Each tenant’s execution path involves three distinct IAM roles, each scoped to a specific function:

Role 1 — SM IAM Role (Step Functions)
Purpose: Allows the Step Functions State Machine to trigger the ECS Fargate task.
Key Permissions:
	∙	ecs:RunTask — scoped to this tenant’s task definition ARN
	∙	iam:PassRole — scoped to this tenant’s ECS Task IAM Role ARN only
Why scoped tightly: The State Machine should only be able to run this tenant’s task, and only pass this tenant’s IAM role. Cross-tenant invocation is denied by default.

Role 2 — ECS Task IAM Role (Runtime)
Purpose: Runtime permissions assumed by the DBT container during execution.
Key Permissions:
	∙	s3:GetObject, s3:PutObject — scoped to tenant S3 buckets only
	∙	logs:CreateLogStream, logs:PutLogEvents — scoped to tenant log group
	∙	ssm:GetParameter — scoped to tenant SSM parameter paths
	∙	Secrets Manager access for DBT profile credentials (if applicable)
Why this matters: This role defines what the DBT code can do at runtime. It should have no access to other tenants’ buckets, log groups, or secrets.

Role 3 — ECS Execution Role (Fargate Infrastructure)
Purpose: Used by Fargate itself (not the container) to pull the image and stream logs.
Key Permissions:
	∙	ecr:GetAuthorizationToken
	∙	ecr:BatchGetImage, ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer — scoped to tenant ECR repository
	∙	logs:CreateLogStream, logs:PutLogEvents
Why separate from Task Role: The Execution Role is an infrastructure concern — pulling images and writing logs. It should never have access to tenant data resources (S3, SSM, databases).

Permission Boundary Diagram

Step Functions (SM IAM Role)
    └── ecs:RunTask → [Tenant Task Definition]
    └── iam:PassRole → [ECS Task IAM Role] only

Fargate Runtime (Execution Role)
    └── ECR pull → [Tenant ECR Repo] only
    └── CloudWatch log stream → [Tenant Log Group] only

DBT Container (ECS Task IAM Role)
    └── S3 → [Tenant Buckets] only
    └── SSM → [Tenant Parameters] only
    └── CloudWatch → [Tenant Log Group] only


📄 4.1 — Multi-Tenancy Model
Labels: multi-tenancy isolation architecture
Parent: Multi-Tenancy

The Model
Each tenant on the platform is an isolated unit. From the moment a tenant is onboarded, every resource in their stack is dedicated to them. No resource is shared with another tenant at the control plane or data plane level.
This is not enforced by tagging or policy alone — it is structural. A tenant’s State Machine can only invoke their task definition. Their task definition uses their IAM role. Their IAM role can only access their S3 buckets and their log group.

Tenant Onboarding Stack
When a new tenant is onboarded, the following is deployed (all dedicated to that tenant):

Tenant A
├── Service Catalog Product (Tenant A)
├── Step Functions State Machine (Tenant A)
├── SM IAM Role (Tenant A)
├── ECS Task Definition (Tenant A)
│   ├── Container Image: Tenant A ECR
│   ├── CPU / Memory: right-sized for Tenant A
│   └── Task Role: Tenant A Task IAM Role
├── ECS Task IAM Role (Tenant A)
├── ECS Execution Role (Tenant A)
├── CloudWatch Log Group (Tenant A)
└── ECR Repository (Tenant A)


Tenant B has an identical structure — completely separate, with no links between the two stacks.

Runtime Isolation
At execution time, Fargate provides the final layer of isolation: each task runs with its own ENI, its own CPU and memory allocation, and an AWS-managed runtime. There is no shared host, no shared network interface, and no shared process space.

What This Means Operationally
	∙	A broken task definition for Tenant A does not affect Tenant B’s runs
	∙	A permission error for Tenant A does not cause Tenant B to fail
	∙	Scaling one tenant’s workload has zero effect on another tenant’s performance
	∙	Each tenant’s logs, metrics, and audit trail are independently queryable

